    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nIf we wish to sum the cubes of the first [MATH: N] integers, an easy way is to use the following formula:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(N)n^{3}|= 1^{3} + 2^{3} + 3^{3} + \u22ef + (N \u2212 1)^{3} + N^{3}; = (1)/(4)N^{2}(N + 1)^{2}]\n\nContent:\nSince we also know that\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(N)n^{2}|= 1^{2} + 2^{2} + 3^{2} + \u22ef + (N \u2212 1)^{2} + N^{2}; = (1)/(6)N(N + 1)(2N + 1)]\nand\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(N)n|= 1 + 2 + 3 + \u22ef + (N \u2212 1) + N; = (1)/(2)N(N + 1),]\n\nContent:\nwe can use these formulae to evaluate any series of the form\n[MATH: \\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^N (an^3+bn^2+cn+d)}.]\n\nContent:\nFinally, it's worth noting that\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\sum_{n=1}^N n^3 = \\frac{1}{4}N^2(N+1)^2 = \\left(\\frac{1}{2}N(N+1)\\right)^2 = \\left(\\sum_{n=1}^N n\\right)^2.]\n\nContent:\nIn other words, the sum of the first [MATH: N] cube numbers is equal to the square of the sum of the first [MATH: N] natural numbers.\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Calculating the Sum of the First N Cube Numbers\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{100} n^3.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nUsing the formula\n\n[MATH: \\sum_{n=1}^N n^3 = \\frac{1}{4}N^2(N+1)^2]\n\nwith [MATH: N=100,] we get\n\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(100)n^{3}|= (1)/(4) \u22c5 100^{2}(100 + 1)^{2}; = (1)/(4) \u22c5 100^{2} \u22c5 101^{2}; = (1)/(4) \u22c5 10000 \u22c5 10201; = 25502500.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nCalculator Instructions:\nA scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(25)n^{3} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 103625]\nb. [MATH: 102625]\nc. [MATH: 106625]\nd. [MATH: 104625]\ne. [MATH: 105625]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nCalculator Instructions:\nA scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(20)n^{3}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 36100]\nb. [MATH: 44100]\nc. [MATH: 41800]\nd. [MATH: 38200]\ne. [MATH: 53361]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Calculating the Sum of a Finite Cubic Series with a Constant Factor\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate\n[MATH: \\displaystyle\n\\sum_{n=1}^{100} 4n^3.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we take the factor [MATH: 4] outside the summation:\n\n[MATH: \\sum_{n=1}^{100} 4n^3 = 4\\sum_{n=1}^{100} n^3]\n\nNow, using the formula\n\n[MATH: \\sum_{n=1}^N n^3 = \\frac{1}{4}N^2(N+1)^2]\n\nwith [MATH: N=100,] we get\n\n[MATH: 4\u2211_(n = 1)^(100)n^{3}|= 4((1)/(4) \u22c5 100^{2}(100 + 1)^{2}); = 4((1)/(4) \u22c5 100^{2} \u22c5 101^{2}); = 4((1)/(4) \u22c5 10000 \u22c5 10201); = 4 \u22c5 25502500; = 102010000.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nCalculator Instructions:\nA scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(30)(1)/(3)n^{3}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 71025]\nb. [MATH: 70375]\nc. [MATH: 72500]\nd. [MATH: 72075]\ne. [MATH: 71850]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nCalculator Instructions:\nA scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(25)4n^{3}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 422800]\nb. [MATH: 422700]\nc. [MATH: 422400]\nd. [MATH: 422600]\ne. [MATH: 422500]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Calculating the Sum of a Cubic Series with a Quadratic, Linear, or Constant Term\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate\n[MATH: \\displaystyle\n\\sum_{n=1}^{50} (3n^3+2n).]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nNotice that we can write the given summation as follows:\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(50)(3n^{3} + 2n)|= \u2211_(n = 1)^(50)3n^{3} + \u2211_(n = 1)^(50)2n; = 3\u2211_(n = 1)^(50)n^{3} + 2\u2211_(n = 1)^(50)n]\n\nNow, let's evaluate the two series using our formulas.\n\n- For the first series, we use the formula\n[MATH: \\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^N n^3 = \\dfrac1 4 N^2 (N+1)^2]\nwith [MATH: N=50,] and we get\n[MATH: 3\u2211_(n = 1)^(50)n^{3}|= 3((1)/(4) \u22c5 50^{2}(50 + 1)^{2}); = 3((1)/(4) \u22c5 50^{2} \u22c5 51^{2}); = 3 \u22c5 1625625; = 4876875.]\n- For the second series, we use the formula\n[MATH: \\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^N n = \\dfrac1 2 N (N+1)]\nwith [MATH: N=50,] and we get\n[MATH: 2\u2211_(n = 1)^(50)n|= 2((1)/(2) \u22c5 50(50 + 1)); = 2 \u22c5 1275; = 2550.]\n\nFinally, we have\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(50)(3n^{3} + 2n)|= 4876875 + 2550; = 4879425.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nCalculator Instructions:\nA scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nPrompt:\nFind [MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(N)(4n^{3} + 3n^{2} + 2n)] in terms of [MATH: N.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (N(N + 1)(2N^{2} \u2212 N + 4))/(2)]\nb. [MATH: (N(N + 1)(N^{2} + N + 1))/(2)]\nc. [MATH: (N(N + 1)(4N^{2} + 2N + 1))/(2)]\nd. [MATH: (N(N + 1)(3N^{2} + 4N \u2212 1))/(2)]\ne. [MATH: (N(N + 1)(2N^{2} + 4N + 3))/(2)]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nCalculator Instructions:\nA scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(30)(2 \u2212 6n^{3}).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 1298400]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 1297290]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 1297350]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 1300140]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 1299340]"
